const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"RC_E10_001","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P1","passage":"Until the early part of the 20th century, American thought on the causes of crime paralleled and mostly reflected European criminological thought. Throughout the 19th century on both continents, the major theories were basically genetic, biological theories. Towards the close of the century, a new but clearly related trend of thought developed in Europe, the bio-anthropological school of Italy. This was an extension of earlier biological thought, imported uncritically for the most part into the United States, which attributed crime to inherited, atavistic, anatomical defects. In the midst of modern Italian civilization, prehistoric men stalked the land in the form of criminals.\\n\\nTo modern students, the original bio-constitutional theories appear quite strange, \\"if not bizarre\\". Modernized biological theories prevail, particularly in Europe, but they have the association of overt stigmata as morphological representation of inherited causal defects and therefore have lost much of the imagery which appears so odd. The strangeness of the originals, as they stand in contrast to more recent thought, stimulate one's curiosity about their origin. Unless one is to make the same fallacy and assume that the early criminologists were \\"possessed\\", one ought to be able to give some account of the theories which understood the criminal as a prehistoric man anachronistically reborn. An explanation of the origin of the theories would be a major undertaking. It would require a thorough familiarity with the historical period and intellectual history of the times. The explanation would also be aided by knowledge about these theorists.\\n\\nFor instance, the pre-Lombrosian, biological theories were written in stratified societies by men who came from well-to-do families, and who were all medical doctors: Kraepelin, Gall, and Prichard, for instance in Europe; and Rush, and Caldwell, in the United States. The men who developed the later bio-constitutional theories (Lombroso, and Garofalo) also had origins in comfortable circumstances and were educated in the professions. These attributes would suggest that theorists' explanations of criminality were perhaps influenced by their relative position in the social order; by the fact that they were moneyed and propertied individuals from the upper class who resided in a society organized to perpetuate the advantages of privilege, power, and wealth, attained through the stratification of people into a hierarchy of classes.\\n\\nIt was as true then as it is today that the vast majority of official criminals came from the lowest social classes. Lombroso, for instance, acknowledged the \\"preponderance of poor criminals\\" and reported occupations which were mainly proletarian or unemployed. Lombroso thought, however, that the occupations which he reported were a little too high because criminals tended to exaggerate their skills. He felt that criminals, along with the American Indians, had a basic \\"aversion to work\\".\\n\\nSince criminologists and criminals arose from opposing social classes, it is not surprising to find that they were adversaries. Crime is often a form of conduct which takes the life, health, property, and wealth of its possessors and is often considered an indirect attack on the social order. The conception of the criminal as a savage is thus the manifestation of the class conflict between criminologists and criminal. It is further expressed in the criminologists' major drive to develop methods for the elimination, correction or control of the criminal, methods which consistently and conveniently fail to involve a fundamental rearrangement in the class structure.","question":"A fundamental conclusion by the author is that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"the criminologists of the early 20th century imposed their personal attitudes on the academic explanations of criminology.","B":"the early biological and genetic theories have essentially been the landmark theories of criminology that have stood the test of time.","C":"the conflict between criminologists and criminals has become a class conflict because they belong to two distinct classes.","D":"some criminologists define crime that present a universal perspective about the criminals, who are considered anti-social."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"the criminologists of the early 20th century imposed their personal attitudes on the academic explanations of criminology.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the passage's central argument is that early criminologists came from privileged classes and their social position distorted their theories, imposing elite class biases on academic criminology. Choice B is incorrect — the passage explicitly says these theories look 'quite strange, if not bizarre' to modern students, implying they have not stood the test of time; this contradicts the passage. Choice C is incorrect — the class conflict between criminologists and criminals is a supporting point in the last paragraph, not the fundamental conclusion; it is a consequence of the broader argument about elite bias. Choice D is incorrect — the passage argues the opposite: early definitions of crime reflected a privileged perspective, not a universal one; this contradicts the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_002","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P1","passage":"Until the early part of the 20th century, American thought on the causes of crime paralleled and mostly reflected European criminological thought. Throughout the 19th century on both continents, the major theories were basically genetic, biological theories. Towards the close of the century, a new but clearly related trend of thought developed in Europe, the bio-anthropological school of Italy. This was an extension of earlier biological thought, imported uncritically for the most part into the United States, which attributed crime to inherited, atavistic, anatomical defects. In the midst of modern Italian civilization, prehistoric men stalked the land in the form of criminals.\\n\\nTo modern students, the original bio-constitutional theories appear quite strange, \\"if not bizarre\\". Modernized biological theories prevail, particularly in Europe, but they have the association of overt stigmata as morphological representation of inherited causal defects and therefore have lost much of the imagery which appears so odd. The strangeness of the originals, as they stand in contrast to more recent thought, stimulate one's curiosity about their origin. Unless one is to make the same fallacy and assume that the early criminologists were \\"possessed\\", one ought to be able to give some account of the theories which understood the criminal as a prehistoric man anachronistically reborn. An explanation of the origin of the theories would be a major undertaking. It would require a thorough familiarity with the historical period and intellectual history of the times. The explanation would also be aided by knowledge about these theorists.\\n\\nFor instance, the pre-Lombrosian, biological theories were written in stratified societies by men who came from well-to-do families, and who were all medical doctors: Kraepelin, Gall, and Prichard, for instance in Europe; and Rush, and Caldwell, in the United States. The men who developed the later bio-constitutional theories (Lombroso, and Garofalo) also had origins in comfortable circumstances and were educated in the professions. These attributes would suggest that theorists' explanations of criminality were perhaps influenced by their relative position in the social order; by the fact that they were moneyed and propertied individuals from the upper class who resided in a society organized to perpetuate the advantages of privilege, power, and wealth, attained through the stratification of people into a hierarchy of classes.\\n\\nIt was as true then as it is today that the vast majority of official criminals came from the lowest social classes. Lombroso, for instance, acknowledged the \\"preponderance of poor criminals\\" and reported occupations which were mainly proletarian or unemployed. Lombroso thought, however, that the occupations which he reported were a little too high because criminals tended to exaggerate their skills. He felt that criminals, along with the American Indians, had a basic \\"aversion to work\\".\\n\\nSince criminologists and criminals arose from opposing social classes, it is not surprising to find that they were adversaries. Crime is often a form of conduct which takes the life, health, property, and wealth of its possessors and is often considered an indirect attack on the social order. The conception of the criminal as a savage is thus the manifestation of the class conflict between criminologists and criminal. It is further expressed in the criminologists' major drive to develop methods for the elimination, correction or control of the criminal, methods which consistently and conveniently fail to involve a fundamental rearrangement in the class structure.","question":"Which of the following sets of words/phrases best encapsulates the issues discussed in the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Biological Theories; Genetic Theories; Criminology; Criminals","B":"Early Criminologists; Personal Bias; Their impact; Academic discussions","C":"Criminology; Earlier Perspectives; Modern Perspectives; Personal Bias","D":"Criminology; Evolution; Early Criminologists; Their contribution."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Early Criminologists; Personal Bias; Their impact; Academic discussions","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — biological and genetic theories are mentioned but are not the central concern; the passage's focus is on the bias behind those theories, not the theories themselves in isolation. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage traces who the early criminologists were, how their privileged background introduced personal bias, how that bias shaped their theories, and what effect this had on academic criminology; all four elements are covered. Choice C is incorrect — modern perspectives of criminology are not discussed in the passage; including this overstates the scope. Choice D is incorrect — 'their contribution' frames the early criminologists positively, but the passage is critical of them; 'evolution' is also too vague to capture the bias argument.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_003","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P1","passage":"Until the early part of the 20th century, American thought on the causes of crime paralleled and mostly reflected European criminological thought. Throughout the 19th century on both continents, the major theories were basically genetic, biological theories. Towards the close of the century, a new but clearly related trend of thought developed in Europe, the bio-anthropological school of Italy. This was an extension of earlier biological thought, imported uncritically for the most part into the United States, which attributed crime to inherited, atavistic, anatomical defects. In the midst of modern Italian civilization, prehistoric men stalked the land in the form of criminals.\\n\\nTo modern students, the original bio-constitutional theories appear quite strange, \\"if not bizarre\\". Modernized biological theories prevail, particularly in Europe, but they have the association of overt stigmata as morphological representation of inherited causal defects and therefore have lost much of the imagery which appears so odd. The strangeness of the originals, as they stand in contrast to more recent thought, stimulate one's curiosity about their origin. Unless one is to make the same fallacy and assume that the early criminologists were \\"possessed\\", one ought to be able to give some account of the theories which understood the criminal as a prehistoric man anachronistically reborn. An explanation of the origin of the theories would be a major undertaking. It would require a thorough familiarity with the historical period and intellectual history of the times. The explanation would also be aided by knowledge about these theorists.\\n\\nFor instance, the pre-Lombrosian, biological theories were written in stratified societies by men who came from well-to-do families, and who were all medical doctors: Kraepelin, Gall, and Prichard, for instance in Europe; and Rush, and Caldwell, in the United States. The men who developed the later bio-constitutional theories (Lombroso, and Garofalo) also had origins in comfortable circumstances and were educated in the professions. These attributes would suggest that theorists' explanations of criminality were perhaps influenced by their relative position in the social order; by the fact that they were moneyed and propertied individuals from the upper class who resided in a society organized to perpetuate the advantages of privilege, power, and wealth, attained through the stratification of people into a hierarchy of classes.\\n\\nIt was as true then as it is today that the vast majority of official criminals came from the lowest social classes. Lombroso, for instance, acknowledged the \\"preponderance of poor criminals\\" and reported occupations which were mainly proletarian or unemployed. Lombroso thought, however, that the occupations which he reported were a little too high because criminals tended to exaggerate their skills. He felt that criminals, along with the American Indians, had a basic \\"aversion to work\\".\\n\\nSince criminologists and criminals arose from opposing social classes, it is not surprising to find that they were adversaries. Crime is often a form of conduct which takes the life, health, property, and wealth of its possessors and is often considered an indirect attack on the social order. The conception of the criminal as a savage is thus the manifestation of the class conflict between criminologists and criminal. It is further expressed in the criminologists' major drive to develop methods for the elimination, correction or control of the criminal, methods which consistently and conveniently fail to involve a fundamental rearrangement in the class structure.","question":"Which of the following is not a valid inference from the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The earlier theories of criminology are out of sync with scientific attitude that is to prevail.","B":"The earlier theories of criminology reflect the desire to consolidate class structure.","C":"Early analysis of criminal behaviour was with the academic objectivity that is essential for an academic discipline.","D":"Criminality was once connected to lack of civility and presence of savageness."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Early analysis of criminal behaviour was with the academic objectivity that is essential for an academic discipline.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — this is a valid inference; the passage says theories look 'quite strange, if not bizarre' to modern students, implying they are out of sync with current scientific thinking. Choice B is incorrect — this is a valid inference from the last paragraph, which states methods for controlling criminals 'consistently and conveniently fail to involve a fundamental rearrangement in the class structure', implying a desire to preserve it. Choice C is CORRECT as the NOT valid inference — the entire passage argues that early criminological analysis was distorted by the class position of the theorists; claiming it was conducted with academic objectivity directly contradicts the passage's central argument. Choice D is incorrect — this is a valid inference; the passage describes criminals as being viewed as 'prehistoric men' and 'savages', directly connecting criminality to lack of civility.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_004","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P1","passage":"Until the early part of the 20th century, American thought on the causes of crime paralleled and mostly reflected European criminological thought. Throughout the 19th century on both continents, the major theories were basically genetic, biological theories. Towards the close of the century, a new but clearly related trend of thought developed in Europe, the bio-anthropological school of Italy. This was an extension of earlier biological thought, imported uncritically for the most part into the United States, which attributed crime to inherited, atavistic, anatomical defects. In the midst of modern Italian civilization, prehistoric men stalked the land in the form of criminals.\\n\\nTo modern students, the original bio-constitutional theories appear quite strange, \\"if not bizarre\\". Modernized biological theories prevail, particularly in Europe, but they have the association of overt stigmata as morphological representation of inherited causal defects and therefore have lost much of the imagery which appears so odd. The strangeness of the originals, as they stand in contrast to more recent thought, stimulate one's curiosity about their origin. Unless one is to make the same fallacy and assume that the early criminologists were \\"possessed\\", one ought to be able to give some account of the theories which understood the criminal as a prehistoric man anachronistically reborn. An explanation of the origin of the theories would be a major undertaking. It would require a thorough familiarity with the historical period and intellectual history of the times. The explanation would also be aided by knowledge about these theorists.\\n\\nFor instance, the pre-Lombrosian, biological theories were written in stratified societies by men who came from well-to-do families, and who were all medical doctors: Kraepelin, Gall, and Prichard, for instance in Europe; and Rush, and Caldwell, in the United States. The men who developed the later bio-constitutional theories (Lombroso, and Garofalo) also had origins in comfortable circumstances and were educated in the professions. These attributes would suggest that theorists' explanations of criminality were perhaps influenced by their relative position in the social order; by the fact that they were moneyed and propertied individuals from the upper class who resided in a society organized to perpetuate the advantages of privilege, power, and wealth, attained through the stratification of people into a hierarchy of classes.\\n\\nIt was as true then as it is today that the vast majority of official criminals came from the lowest social classes. Lombroso, for instance, acknowledged the \\"preponderance of poor criminals\\" and reported occupations which were mainly proletarian or unemployed. Lombroso thought, however, that the occupations which he reported were a little too high because criminals tended to exaggerate their skills. He felt that criminals, along with the American Indians, had a basic \\"aversion to work\\".\\n\\nSince criminologists and criminals arose from opposing social classes, it is not surprising to find that they were adversaries. Crime is often a form of conduct which takes the life, health, property, and wealth of its possessors and is often considered an indirect attack on the social order. The conception of the criminal as a savage is thus the manifestation of the class conflict between criminologists and criminal. It is further expressed in the criminologists' major drive to develop methods for the elimination, correction or control of the criminal, methods which consistently and conveniently fail to involve a fundamental rearrangement in the class structure.","question":"To resolve the issue mentioned in the passage, which of the following courses of actions is preferable?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"We should uncritically accept bio-anthropological school of Italy in defining concepts of criminology.","B":"We should totally ignore the concepts present by biological, genetic theories that dominated the early period.","C":"Bio-constitutional theories of Lombroso, and Garofalo are to be considered favourably so that their concepts are applied in modern criminology.","D":"To understand the genesis of a crime and ways to prevent it, the socio-cultural influences acting on the criminal need to be understood."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"To understand the genesis of a crime and ways to prevent it, the socio-cultural influences acting on the criminal need to be understood.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"too_extreme","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage explicitly criticises the uncritical import of the bio-anthropological school; adopting it uncritically is the very problem the passage identifies; this contradicts the passage. Choice B is incorrect — total rejection is too extreme; the passage calls for rational understanding of those theories to explain their origins, not wholesale dismissal. Choice C is incorrect — the passage is critical of Lombroso and Garofalo's biased perspectives; applying their concepts favourably repeats the same error the passage critiques. Choice D is CORRECT — the passage's argument is that early theories ignored socio-economic context; the corrective implied throughout is to understand the social and cultural influences that actually drive criminal behaviour.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_005","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P1","passage":"Until the early part of the 20th century, American thought on the causes of crime paralleled and mostly reflected European criminological thought. Throughout the 19th century on both continents, the major theories were basically genetic, biological theories. Towards the close of the century, a new but clearly related trend of thought developed in Europe, the bio-anthropological school of Italy. This was an extension of earlier biological thought, imported uncritically for the most part into the United States, which attributed crime to inherited, atavistic, anatomical defects. In the midst of modern Italian civilization, prehistoric men stalked the land in the form of criminals.\\n\\nTo modern students, the original bio-constitutional theories appear quite strange, \\"if not bizarre\\". Modernized biological theories prevail, particularly in Europe, but they have the association of overt stigmata as morphological representation of inherited causal defects and therefore have lost much of the imagery which appears so odd. The strangeness of the originals, as they stand in contrast to more recent thought, stimulate one's curiosity about their origin. Unless one is to make the same fallacy and assume that the early criminologists were \\"possessed\\", one ought to be able to give some account of the theories which understood the criminal as a prehistoric man anachronistically reborn. An explanation of the origin of the theories would be a major undertaking. It would require a thorough familiarity with the historical period and intellectual history of the times. The explanation would also be aided by knowledge about these theorists.\\n\\nFor instance, the pre-Lombrosian, biological theories were written in stratified societies by men who came from well-to-do families, and who were all medical doctors: Kraepelin, Gall, and Prichard, for instance in Europe; and Rush, and Caldwell, in the United States. The men who developed the later bio-constitutional theories (Lombroso, and Garofalo) also had origins in comfortable circumstances and were educated in the professions. These attributes would suggest that theorists' explanations of criminality were perhaps influenced by their relative position in the social order; by the fact that they were moneyed and propertied individuals from the upper class who resided in a society organized to perpetuate the advantages of privilege, power, and wealth, attained through the stratification of people into a hierarchy of classes.\\n\\nIt was as true then as it is today that the vast majority of official criminals came from the lowest social classes. Lombroso, for instance, acknowledged the \\"preponderance of poor criminals\\" and reported occupations which were mainly proletarian or unemployed. Lombroso thought, however, that the occupations which he reported were a little too high because criminals tended to exaggerate their skills. He felt that criminals, along with the American Indians, had a basic \\"aversion to work\\".\\n\\nSince criminologists and criminals arose from opposing social classes, it is not surprising to find that they were adversaries. Crime is often a form of conduct which takes the life, health, property, and wealth of its possessors and is often considered an indirect attack on the social order. The conception of the criminal as a savage is thus the manifestation of the class conflict between criminologists and criminal. It is further expressed in the criminologists' major drive to develop methods for the elimination, correction or control of the criminal, methods which consistently and conveniently fail to involve a fundamental rearrangement in the class structure.","question":"The author notes that 'Unless one is to make the same fallacy and assume that the early criminologists were \\"possessed\\", one ought to be able to give some account of the theories which understood the criminal as a prehistoric man anachronistically reborn'. Which one of the following is the point the author is trying to make?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"We need to understand that the original theories of criminology are fallacious and thus, they seem strange to modern students.","B":"We need to understand that the original theories seem strange, even bizarre, to modern students, and hence they are fallacious.","C":"We should not give weightage to the original theories of criminology because they were biased views of early criminologists who were from elite groups.","D":"We need to opt for a rational approach to understanding the earlier theories, rather than relying on unfounded assumptions."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"We need to opt for a rational approach to understanding the earlier theories, rather than relying on unfounded assumptions.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","B":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","C":"too_extreme"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage does not establish a causal link from being fallacious to seeming strange; strangeness is not evidence of fallaciousness; this confuses the relationship. Choice B is incorrect — this reverses the same faulty logic as A; seeming strange does not make theories fallacious; the author warns against exactly this kind of reasoning. Choice C is incorrect — the author calls for rational understanding of the theories, not dismissal; 'should not give weightage' is too extreme and contradicts the passage's call to 'give some account of the theories'. Choice D is CORRECT — the author's point is that rather than dismissing early theories with an unexamined assumption ('they were possessed'), we should rationally examine their origins and intellectual context; this is the call for a rational, non-fallacious approach.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_006","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P2","passage":"The debate about price gouging that arose in the aftermath of Hurricane Charley raises hard questions of morality and law: Is it wrong for sellers to take advantage of a natural disaster by charging whatever the market will bear? If so, what, if anything, should the law do about it? Should the state prohibit price gouging, even if doing so interferes with the freedom of buyers and sellers to make whatever deals they choose?\\n\\nThese questions are not only about how individuals should treat one another. They are also about what the law should be, and how society should be organized. They are questions about justice. To answer them, we have to explore the meaning of justice. If we look closely at the price-gouging debate, the arguments for and against price-gouging laws revolve around three ideas: maximizing welfare, respecting freedom and promoting virtue; each of these ideas points to a different way of thinking about justice.\\n\\nThe standard case for unfettered markets rests on two claims – one about welfare, the other about freedom. First, markets promote the welfare of society as a whole by providing incentives for people to work hard supplying the goods that other people want. Second, markets respect individual freedom; rather than impose a certain value on goods and services, markets let people choose for themselves what value to place on the things they exchange.\\n\\nNor surprisingly, the opponents of price-gouging laws invoke these two familiar arguments for free markets. How do defenders of price gouging laws respond? First, they argue that the welfare of society as whole is not really served by the exorbitant prices charged in hard times. Even if high prices call forth a greater supply of goods, this benefit has to be weighed against the burden such prices impose on those least able to afford them. For the affluent, paying inflated prices for a gallon of gas or a motel room in a storm may be an annoyance; but for those of modest means, such prices pose a genuine hardship, one that might lead them to stay in harm's way rather than flee to safety. Proponents of price gouging laws argue that any estimate of the general welfare must include the pain and suffering of those who may be priced out of basic necessities during an emergency.\\n\\nSecond, defenders of price-gouging laws maintain that, under certain conditions, the free market is not truly free. As Crist points out, \\"buyers under duress have no freedom. Their purchase of necessities like safe lodging are forced.\\" If you are fleeing a hurricane with your family, the exorbitant price you pay for gas or shelter is not really a voluntary exchange. It's something closer to extortion. So, to decide whether price-gouging laws are justified, we need to assess these competing accounts of welfare and of freedom.","question":"Which of the following sets of words/phrases best encapsulates the issues discussed in the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Price-gouging; its economics; its sociology; the reconciliation.","B":"Price-gouging; economic perspective; social perspective; ethical perspective","C":"Price-gouging; free-markets; welfare of citizens; the inherent conflict","D":"Price-gouging; effect of disasters; legal protection; ethical concerns"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Price-gouging; free-markets; welfare of citizens; the inherent conflict","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — 'the reconciliation' is beyond the scope; the passage presents competing arguments but does not resolve them. Choice B is incorrect — the passage does not specifically discuss an ethical perspective as a distinct strand; it frames the debate in terms of welfare and freedom, not ethics separately. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage centres on price gouging as a phenomenon, the free market arguments on both sides, the welfare of citizens as the contested ground, and the inherent conflict between the two positions. Choice D is incorrect — while Hurricane Charley is the trigger, the effect of disasters per se is not the focus; 'legal protection' and 'ethical concerns' are present but not the primary framing.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_007","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P2","passage":"The debate about price gouging that arose in the aftermath of Hurricane Charley raises hard questions of morality and law: Is it wrong for sellers to take advantage of a natural disaster by charging whatever the market will bear? If so, what, if anything, should the law do about it? Should the state prohibit price gouging, even if doing so interferes with the freedom of buyers and sellers to make whatever deals they choose?\\n\\nThese questions are not only about how individuals should treat one another. They are also about what the law should be, and how society should be organized. They are questions about justice. To answer them, we have to explore the meaning of justice. If we look closely at the price-gouging debate, the arguments for and against price-gouging laws revolve around three ideas: maximizing welfare, respecting freedom and promoting virtue; each of these ideas points to a different way of thinking about justice.\\n\\nThe standard case for unfettered markets rests on two claims – one about welfare, the other about freedom. First, markets promote the welfare of society as a whole by providing incentives for people to work hard supplying the goods that other people want. Second, markets respect individual freedom; rather than impose a certain value on goods and services, markets let people choose for themselves what value to place on the things they exchange.\\n\\nNor surprisingly, the opponents of price-gouging laws invoke these two familiar arguments for free markets. How do defenders of price gouging laws respond? First, they argue that the welfare of society as whole is not really served by the exorbitant prices charged in hard times. Even if high prices call forth a greater supply of goods, this benefit has to be weighed against the burden such prices impose on those least able to afford them. For the affluent, paying inflated prices for a gallon of gas or a motel room in a storm may be an annoyance; but for those of modest means, such prices pose a genuine hardship, one that might lead them to stay in harm's way rather than flee to safety. Proponents of price gouging laws argue that any estimate of the general welfare must include the pain and suffering of those who may be priced out of basic necessities during an emergency.\\n\\nSecond, defenders of price-gouging laws maintain that, under certain conditions, the free market is not truly free. As Crist points out, \\"buyers under duress have no freedom. Their purchase of necessities like safe lodging are forced.\\" If you are fleeing a hurricane with your family, the exorbitant price you pay for gas or shelter is not really a voluntary exchange. It's something closer to extortion. So, to decide whether price-gouging laws are justified, we need to assess these competing accounts of welfare and of freedom.","question":"The case for 'unfettered markets' assumes that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"wealth is equally distributed among all people who need to pay inflated prices during a disaster.","B":"the number of people who produce goods and services is more than that consuming them.","C":"the inflated prices have a net cost on the society that is less than the net benefit it results in.","D":"the prices during the disaster are invariably higher than those after the disaster."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"the inflated prices have a net cost on the society that is less than the net benefit it results in.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the unfettered market argument does not assume equal wealth distribution; in fact, the proponents of price gouging laws point out the unequal burden as a counter-argument; this is irrelevant to the unfettered market assumption. Choice B is incorrect — supply-demand ratios are not the stated assumption; this is out of scope. Choice C is CORRECT — the unfettered market argument rests on the claim that markets promote overall welfare; for this to hold, the net benefit of high prices (incentivising supply) must exceed the net cost (burden on those who cannot afford them); this is the unstated but necessary assumption. Choice D is incorrect — price comparisons across time are not part of the unfettered market argument; this is irrelevant.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_008","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P2","passage":"The debate about price gouging that arose in the aftermath of Hurricane Charley raises hard questions of morality and law: Is it wrong for sellers to take advantage of a natural disaster by charging whatever the market will bear? If so, what, if anything, should the law do about it? Should the state prohibit price gouging, even if doing so interferes with the freedom of buyers and sellers to make whatever deals they choose?\\n\\nThese questions are not only about how individuals should treat one another. They are also about what the law should be, and how society should be organized. They are questions about justice. To answer them, we have to explore the meaning of justice. If we look closely at the price-gouging debate, the arguments for and against price-gouging laws revolve around three ideas: maximizing welfare, respecting freedom and promoting virtue; each of these ideas points to a different way of thinking about justice.\\n\\nThe standard case for unfettered markets rests on two claims – one about welfare, the other about freedom. First, markets promote the welfare of society as a whole by providing incentives for people to work hard supplying the goods that other people want. Second, markets respect individual freedom; rather than impose a certain value on goods and services, markets let people choose for themselves what value to place on the things they exchange.\\n\\nNor surprisingly, the opponents of price-gouging laws invoke these two familiar arguments for free markets. How do defenders of price gouging laws respond? First, they argue that the welfare of society as whole is not really served by the exorbitant prices charged in hard times. Even if high prices call forth a greater supply of goods, this benefit has to be weighed against the burden such prices impose on those least able to afford them. For the affluent, paying inflated prices for a gallon of gas or a motel room in a storm may be an annoyance; but for those of modest means, such prices pose a genuine hardship, one that might lead them to stay in harm's way rather than flee to safety. Proponents of price gouging laws argue that any estimate of the general welfare must include the pain and suffering of those who may be priced out of basic necessities during an emergency.\\n\\nSecond, defenders of price-gouging laws maintain that, under certain conditions, the free market is not truly free. As Crist points out, \\"buyers under duress have no freedom. Their purchase of necessities like safe lodging are forced.\\" If you are fleeing a hurricane with your family, the exorbitant price you pay for gas or shelter is not really a voluntary exchange. It's something closer to extortion. So, to decide whether price-gouging laws are justified, we need to assess these competing accounts of welfare and of freedom.","question":"Which of the following, if negated, adds most to the strength of the argument of the proponents of price-gouging laws?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Economic benefit for the whole society increases when goods and services are sold at exorbitant prices.","B":"Even affluent people face the hardship of exorbitant prices even when there is no hardship.","C":"Governments of developed countries do not take care of the basic necessities of people when there is a crisis.","D":"Governments in most countries pass laws against monopoly of a company in a specific sector."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Economic benefit for the whole society increases when goods and services are sold at exorbitant prices.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the proponents of price-gouging laws argue that exorbitant prices do NOT serve the welfare of society as a whole; if we negate A ('economic benefit does NOT increase when goods are sold at exorbitant prices'), this directly supports the proponents' central welfare argument, strengthening it. Choice B is incorrect — if negated ('affluent people do NOT face hardship from exorbitant prices'), this actually weakens the proponents' argument by distinguishing the affluent from those of modest means, making the burden less universal; this does not strengthen the proponents' case. Choice C is incorrect — government provision of necessities is not a part of the proponents' argument in the passage; this is irrelevant. Choice D is incorrect — anti-monopoly laws are not discussed in the context of price-gouging arguments; this is out of scope.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_009","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P2","passage":"The debate about price gouging that arose in the aftermath of Hurricane Charley raises hard questions of morality and law: Is it wrong for sellers to take advantage of a natural disaster by charging whatever the market will bear? If so, what, if anything, should the law do about it? Should the state prohibit price gouging, even if doing so interferes with the freedom of buyers and sellers to make whatever deals they choose?\\n\\nThese questions are not only about how individuals should treat one another. They are also about what the law should be, and how society should be organized. They are questions about justice. To answer them, we have to explore the meaning of justice. If we look closely at the price-gouging debate, the arguments for and against price-gouging laws revolve around three ideas: maximizing welfare, respecting freedom and promoting virtue; each of these ideas points to a different way of thinking about justice.\\n\\nThe standard case for unfettered markets rests on two claims – one about welfare, the other about freedom. First, markets promote the welfare of society as a whole by providing incentives for people to work hard supplying the goods that other people want. Second, markets respect individual freedom; rather than impose a certain value on goods and services, markets let people choose for themselves what value to place on the things they exchange.\\n\\nNor surprisingly, the opponents of price-gouging laws invoke these two familiar arguments for free markets. How do defenders of price gouging laws respond? First, they argue that the welfare of society as whole is not really served by the exorbitant prices charged in hard times. Even if high prices call forth a greater supply of goods, this benefit has to be weighed against the burden such prices impose on those least able to afford them. For the affluent, paying inflated prices for a gallon of gas or a motel room in a storm may be an annoyance; but for those of modest means, such prices pose a genuine hardship, one that might lead them to stay in harm's way rather than flee to safety. Proponents of price gouging laws argue that any estimate of the general welfare must include the pain and suffering of those who may be priced out of basic necessities during an emergency.\\n\\nSecond, defenders of price-gouging laws maintain that, under certain conditions, the free market is not truly free. As Crist points out, \\"buyers under duress have no freedom. Their purchase of necessities like safe lodging are forced.\\" If you are fleeing a hurricane with your family, the exorbitant price you pay for gas or shelter is not really a voluntary exchange. It's something closer to extortion. So, to decide whether price-gouging laws are justified, we need to assess these competing accounts of welfare and of freedom.","question":"The arguments of both proponents and opponents of price-gouging laws make use of all these concepts except","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Whether economic interests of people buying or selling services and goods are protected or not.","B":"Whether the laws in question work to the welfare of people involved in the transactions related to goods and services.","C":"Whether it is ethically supportable to capitalize on the desperation of the people in need of services and goods.","D":"Whether price-gouging offers undue advantage to any of the parties involved in the transactions related to goods and services."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Whether it is ethically supportable to capitalize on the desperation of the people in need of services and goods.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — both sides address economic interests; opponents argue free markets protect economic interests through incentives, proponents argue the economic burden on the poor must be included. Choice B is incorrect — welfare of people involved is central to both arguments; opponents claim free markets maximise welfare, proponents argue they harm those of modest means. Choice C is CORRECT as the EXCEPT — while the ethics of capitalising on desperation is implied in the passage's opening framing, neither the proponents nor opponents explicitly invoke ethical supportability as a formal argument; their arguments are framed in terms of welfare and freedom, not ethical judgement per se. Choice D is incorrect — both sides address undue advantage; opponents argue no undue advantage exists in free markets, proponents argue sellers gain undue advantage over desperate buyers.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_010","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P2","passage":"The debate about price gouging that arose in the aftermath of Hurricane Charley raises hard questions of morality and law: Is it wrong for sellers to take advantage of a natural disaster by charging whatever the market will bear? If so, what, if anything, should the law do about it? Should the state prohibit price gouging, even if doing so interferes with the freedom of buyers and sellers to make whatever deals they choose?\\n\\nThese questions are not only about how individuals should treat one another. They are also about what the law should be, and how society should be organized. They are questions about justice. To answer them, we have to explore the meaning of justice. If we look closely at the price-gouging debate, the arguments for and against price-gouging laws revolve around three ideas: maximizing welfare, respecting freedom and promoting virtue; each of these ideas points to a different way of thinking about justice.\\n\\nThe standard case for unfettered markets rests on two claims – one about welfare, the other about freedom. First, markets promote the welfare of society as a whole by providing incentives for people to work hard supplying the goods that other people want. Second, markets respect individual freedom; rather than impose a certain value on goods and services, markets let people choose for themselves what value to place on the things they exchange.\\n\\nNor surprisingly, the opponents of price-gouging laws invoke these two familiar arguments for free markets. How do defenders of price gouging laws respond? First, they argue that the welfare of society as whole is not really served by the exorbitant prices charged in hard times. Even if high prices call forth a greater supply of goods, this benefit has to be weighed against the burden such prices impose on those least able to afford them. For the affluent, paying inflated prices for a gallon of gas or a motel room in a storm may be an annoyance; but for those of modest means, such prices pose a genuine hardship, one that might lead them to stay in harm's way rather than flee to safety. Proponents of price gouging laws argue that any estimate of the general welfare must include the pain and suffering of those who may be priced out of basic necessities during an emergency.\\n\\nSecond, defenders of price-gouging laws maintain that, under certain conditions, the free market is not truly free. As Crist points out, \\"buyers under duress have no freedom. Their purchase of necessities like safe lodging are forced.\\" If you are fleeing a hurricane with your family, the exorbitant price you pay for gas or shelter is not really a voluntary exchange. It's something closer to extortion. So, to decide whether price-gouging laws are justified, we need to assess these competing accounts of welfare and of freedom.","question":"Crist's statement that \\"buyers under duress have no freedom\\" is to counter which point of the opposing argument?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Markets afford individuals the autonomy to determine the worth of the goods and services they exchange.","B":"Markets promote the welfare of society as a whole by providing incentives for people to produce goods and services.","C":"The welfare of society as whole is not really served by the exorbitant prices charged in hard times.","D":"The concepts of welfare and of freedom are the two contrasting concepts that we need to consider while justifying price-gouging laws."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Markets afford individuals the autonomy to determine the worth of the goods and services they exchange.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"irrelevant_comparison","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the opponents of price-gouging laws argue that free markets 'let people choose for themselves what value to place on the things they exchange' (individual freedom/autonomy); Crist's statement directly counters this by arguing that buyers under duress have no real freedom to choose; the transaction is coerced, not voluntary. Choice B is incorrect — Crist's statement is about freedom, not welfare; countering the welfare incentive argument is a different rebuttal made elsewhere in the passage. Choice C is incorrect — this is actually a point made by the proponents of price-gouging laws, not the opposing argument; Crist is supporting the proponents' position, not countering it. Choice D is incorrect — this describes the framework of the debate, not a specific argument to be countered; it is out of scope as a specific point.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_011","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P3","passage":"When we think of medical breakthroughs, our minds often turn to new drugs and treatments. However, innovations in biomaterials and medicine delivery processes are just as revolutionary and important as they pave the way for such advances. A major focus is developing polymers to deliver drugs continuously and at controlled rates for prolonged periods of time. In our research, we have created biomaterials and processes for everything from controlled drug release to tissue regeneration, and worked on anticancer drugs, gene therapy agents and vaccines alike.\\n\\nThis means a better response to some of the limitations of human body, and particularly its central nervous system. When we take a drug orally, it can wreak havoc on the body as it carries medicine throughout our system. By engineering polymers that offer smart delivery systems, we can target specific parts of the body. This limits exposure and therefore adverse effects, offering more effective and precise treatment.\\n\\nA good example of this is treating brain disorders. Drugs taken orally—such as L-DOPA, a dopamine precursor used to treat Parkinson's disease, and Prozac, used to boost serotonin levels in patients with depression—can interact with neurotransmitters. These drugs have side effects because they act throughout the brain. What we can do instead is deliver medicine through a miniaturized cannula — a thin tube used to deliver medicine — that is connected to small pumps implanted under the skin. This technique can deliver tiny doses (on order of hundreds of nanoliters) into brain regions as small as one cubic millimeter, giving us much more control over where medication is dispensed, according to an individual patient's needs.\\n\\nAnother exciting application of biomedical and technological innovation is telemedicine, or medical treatment from a distance. One example of this involves creating miniaturized wireless devices to treat chronic diseases or conditions that require frequent injections. Edible microchips could respond to wireless transmissions, and release drugs at sequential intervals into someone's body to treat diseases. In theory, one could have a \\"pharmacy on a chip\\" that could dispense multiple drugs in regular and accurate doses.\\n\\nThat being said, the fruits of these labors do take some time. Regardless of the research and innovation at hand, clinical trials and funding can have significant and unpredictable effects on timelines for medical innovation and adoption. This makes it even more crucial that we encourage innovation and support scientists and engineers. They need the freedom to explore, learn and create. They must feel that there are no limits to what they can do, that they are doing something important; and we need to raise the profile of innovative new technologies so that other teams of scientists can develop further treatments.\\n\\nFor most diseases and illnesses, we're still at a stage where finding the right treatment is a process of trial and error; most patients are prescribed treatments because they have been proven to work for the majority, and if these don't work then it becomes a long and costly process of finding the one that is effective. But, as the developments in the areas above show, scientists and engineers are beginning to make great strides in making medicine more personal.","question":"All of the following have been discussed in the passage EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Precision Medicine","B":"Narrowly targeted drug delivery","C":"Microchip Fabrication","D":"Tissue engineering"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Microchip Fabrication","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — precision medicine is discussed; the passage discusses targeted drug delivery and personalised treatment throughout. Choice B is incorrect — narrowly targeted drug delivery is explicitly discussed; the cannula delivering nanoliter doses into specific brain regions is a prime example. Choice C is CORRECT as the EXCEPT — while edible microchips are mentioned as a drug delivery vehicle, how they are fabricated is never discussed; the passage treats them as a given tool, not as a fabrication challenge. Choice D is incorrect — tissue regeneration is explicitly mentioned in the first paragraph as part of the research described.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E10_012","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P3","passage":"When we think of medical breakthroughs, our minds often turn to new drugs and treatments. However, innovations in biomaterials and medicine delivery processes are just as revolutionary and important as they pave the way for such advances. A major focus is developing polymers to deliver drugs continuously and at controlled rates for prolonged periods of time. In our research, we have created biomaterials and processes for everything from controlled drug release to tissue regeneration, and worked on anticancer drugs, gene therapy agents and vaccines alike.\\n\\nThis means a better response to some of the limitations of human body, and particularly its central nervous system. When we take a drug orally, it can wreak havoc on the body as it carries medicine throughout our system. By engineering polymers that offer smart delivery systems, we can target specific parts of the body. This limits exposure and therefore adverse effects, offering more effective and precise treatment.\\n\\nA good example of this is treating brain disorders. Drugs taken orally—such as L-DOPA, a dopamine precursor used to treat Parkinson's disease, and Prozac, used to boost serotonin levels in patients with depression—can interact with neurotransmitters. These drugs have side effects because they act throughout the brain. What we can do instead is deliver medicine through a miniaturized cannula — a thin tube used to deliver medicine — that is connected to small pumps implanted under the skin. This technique can deliver tiny doses (on order of hundreds of nanoliters) into brain regions as small as one cubic millimeter, giving us much more control over where medication is dispensed, according to an individual patient's needs.\\n\\nAnother exciting application of biomedical and technological innovation is telemedicine, or medical treatment from a distance. One example of this involves creating miniaturized wireless devices to treat chronic diseases or conditions that require frequent injections. Edible microchips could respond to wireless transmissions, and release drugs at sequential intervals into someone's body to treat diseases. In theory, one could have a \\"pharmacy on a chip\\" that could dispense multiple drugs in regular and accurate doses.\\n\\nThat being said, the fruits of these labors do take some time. Regardless of the research and innovation at hand, clinical trials and funding can have significant and unpredictable effects on timelines for medical innovation and adoption. This makes it even more crucial that we encourage innovation and support scientists and engineers. They need the freedom to explore, learn and create. They must feel that there are no limits to what they can do, that they are doing something important; and we need to raise the profile of innovative new technologies so that other teams of scientists can develop further treatments.\\n\\nFor most diseases and illnesses, we're still at a stage where finding the right treatment is a process of trial and error; most patients are prescribed treatments because they have been proven to work for the majority, and if these don't work then it becomes a long and costly process of finding the one that is effective. But, as the developments in the areas above show, scientists and engineers are beginning to make great strides in making medicine more personal.","question":"It can be inferred from the passage that a \\"pharmacy on a chip\\"","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"could adapt treatments in response to changing conditions in a patient's body.","B":"is programmed to wirelessly release the allotted daily dose automatically.","C":"contains discrete doses of hormones on the inside.","D":"is activated through the application of a small electrical current."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"is programmed to wirelessly release the allotted daily dose automatically.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — adaptive treatment in response to changing body conditions is not described for the pharmacy on a chip; the passage describes it as releasing drugs at sequential intervals, not adaptively. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage states edible microchips 'could respond to wireless transmissions, and release drugs at sequential intervals'; combining wireless response with sequential dosing matches 'programmed to wirelessly release the allotted dose automatically'. Choice C is incorrect — the passage does not specify hormones as the contents; the microchip is described as dispensing 'multiple drugs'; specifying hormones is out of scope. Choice D is incorrect — electrical current activation is not mentioned; the chip responds to wireless transmissions, not electrical current.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_013","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P3","passage":"When we think of medical breakthroughs, our minds often turn to new drugs and treatments. However, innovations in biomaterials and medicine delivery processes are just as revolutionary and important as they pave the way for such advances. A major focus is developing polymers to deliver drugs continuously and at controlled rates for prolonged periods of time. In our research, we have created biomaterials and processes for everything from controlled drug release to tissue regeneration, and worked on anticancer drugs, gene therapy agents and vaccines alike.\\n\\nThis means a better response to some of the limitations of human body, and particularly its central nervous system. When we take a drug orally, it can wreak havoc on the body as it carries medicine throughout our system. By engineering polymers that offer smart delivery systems, we can target specific parts of the body. This limits exposure and therefore adverse effects, offering more effective and precise treatment.\\n\\nA good example of this is treating brain disorders. Drugs taken orally—such as L-DOPA, a dopamine precursor used to treat Parkinson's disease, and Prozac, used to boost serotonin levels in patients with depression—can interact with neurotransmitters. These drugs have side effects because they act throughout the brain. What we can do instead is deliver medicine through a miniaturized cannula — a thin tube used to deliver medicine — that is connected to small pumps implanted under the skin. This technique can deliver tiny doses (on order of hundreds of nanoliters) into brain regions as small as one cubic millimeter, giving us much more control over where medication is dispensed, according to an individual patient's needs.\\n\\nAnother exciting application of biomedical and technological innovation is telemedicine, or medical treatment from a distance. One example of this involves creating miniaturized wireless devices to treat chronic diseases or conditions that require frequent injections. Edible microchips could respond to wireless transmissions, and release drugs at sequential intervals into someone's body to treat diseases. In theory, one could have a \\"pharmacy on a chip\\" that could dispense multiple drugs in regular and accurate doses.\\n\\nThat being said, the fruits of these labors do take some time. Regardless of the research and innovation at hand, clinical trials and funding can have significant and unpredictable effects on timelines for medical innovation and adoption. This makes it even more crucial that we encourage innovation and support scientists and engineers. They need the freedom to explore, learn and create. They must feel that there are no limits to what they can do, that they are doing something important; and we need to raise the profile of innovative new technologies so that other teams of scientists can develop further treatments.\\n\\nFor most diseases and illnesses, we're still at a stage where finding the right treatment is a process of trial and error; most patients are prescribed treatments because they have been proven to work for the majority, and if these don't work then it becomes a long and costly process of finding the one that is effective. But, as the developments in the areas above show, scientists and engineers are beginning to make great strides in making medicine more personal.","question":"All of the following can be inferred from the passage EXCEPT that:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Polymers that deliver medicines do not have unintended effects.","B":"Medicines that treat brain disorders via nanotechnology do not interact with neurotransmitters.","C":"Edible microchips enable real-time tracking of patient health.","D":"Smart pills is an indicator of patient compliance with proper use of medication."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Smart pills is an indicator of patient compliance with proper use of medication.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","C":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — this is inferrable; the passage states polymer smart delivery 'limits exposure and therefore adverse effects', implying reduced unintended effects. Choice B is incorrect — this is inferrable; the passage states oral drugs 'interact with neurotransmitters' and have side effects 'because they act throughout the brain'; the targeted cannula delivery bypasses this by delivering to specific brain regions, implying such nanotechnology-delivered medicines do not interact broadly with neurotransmitters. Choice C is incorrect — this is inferrable; edible microchips responding to wireless transmissions and releasing drugs at sequential intervals implies some form of remote monitoring or health tracking capability. Choice D is CORRECT as the EXCEPT — patient compliance with medication is never discussed in the passage; the passage discusses drug delivery mechanisms, not whether patients take their medication as prescribed; this is entirely out of scope.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_014","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P3","passage":"When we think of medical breakthroughs, our minds often turn to new drugs and treatments. However, innovations in biomaterials and medicine delivery processes are just as revolutionary and important as they pave the way for such advances. A major focus is developing polymers to deliver drugs continuously and at controlled rates for prolonged periods of time. In our research, we have created biomaterials and processes for everything from controlled drug release to tissue regeneration, and worked on anticancer drugs, gene therapy agents and vaccines alike.\\n\\nThis means a better response to some of the limitations of human body, and particularly its central nervous system. When we take a drug orally, it can wreak havoc on the body as it carries medicine throughout our system. By engineering polymers that offer smart delivery systems, we can target specific parts of the body. This limits exposure and therefore adverse effects, offering more effective and precise treatment.\\n\\nA good example of this is treating brain disorders. Drugs taken orally—such as L-DOPA, a dopamine precursor used to treat Parkinson's disease, and Prozac, used to boost serotonin levels in patients with depression—can interact with neurotransmitters. These drugs have side effects because they pact throughout the brain. What we can do instead is deliver medicine through a miniaturized cannula — a thin tube used to deliver medicine — that is connected to small pumps implanted under the skin. This technique can deliver tiny doses (on order of hundreds of nanoliters) into brain regions as small as one cubic millimeter, giving us much more control over where medication is dispensed, according to an individual patient's needs.\\n\\nAnother exciting application of biomedical and technological innovation is telemedicine, or medical treatment from a distance. One example of this involves creating miniaturized wireless devices to treat chronic diseases or conditions that require frequent injections. Edible microchips could respond to wireless transmissions, and release drugs at sequential intervals into someone's body to treat diseases. In theory, one could have a \\"pharmacy on a chip\\" that could dispense multiple drugs in regular and accurate doses.\\n\\nThat being said, the fruits of these labors do take some time. Regardless of the research and innovation at hand, clinical trials and funding can have significant and unpredictable effects on timelines for medical innovation and adoption. This makes it even more crucial that we encourage innovation and support scientists and engineers. They need the freedom to explore, learn and create. They must feel that there are no limits to what they can do, that they are doing something important; and we need to raise the profile of innovative new technologies so that other teams of scientists can develop further treatments.\\n\\nFor most diseases and illnesses, we're still at a stage where finding the right treatment is a process of trial and error; most patients are prescribed treatments because they have been proven to work for the majority, and if these don't work then it becomes a long and costly process of finding the one that is effective. But, as the developments in the areas above show, scientists and engineers are beginning to make great strides in making medicine more personal.","question":"Of the following sets of concepts, identify the set that is conceptually closest to the concerns of the passage.","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Nanotechnology, Medicine, Chip Pharmacy, Innovation","B":"Biomaterials, Medicine Delivery, Personalized Medicine, Innovation","C":"Medical breakthroughs, Innovation, Neurology, Trial and Error","D":"Biotechnology, Drug Delivery, Tissue Engineering, Personalized Medicine"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Biomaterials, Medicine Delivery, Personalized Medicine, Innovation","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — nanotechnology is hinted at but not a named focus; 'chip pharmacy' is a single example, not a central concept; this set is too narrow and specific. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage opens with biomaterials and medicine delivery as its primary focus, builds toward personalized medicine as the goal, and frames innovation as the necessary enabler; all four elements are central and accurate. Choice C is incorrect — 'neurology' is too narrow (only one paragraph addresses brain disorders); 'trial and error' is a minor closing observation, not a central concern. Choice D is incorrect — 'biotechnology' is broader than the passage's focus on biomaterials; tissue engineering is mentioned only briefly as one application, not a central theme.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_015","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P3","passage":"When we think of medical breakthroughs, our minds often turn to new drugs and treatments. However, innovations in biomaterials and medicine delivery processes are just as revolutionary and important as they pave the way for such advances. A major focus is developing polymers to deliver drugs continuously and at controlled rates for prolonged periods of time. In our research, we have created biomaterials and processes for everything from controlled drug release to tissue regeneration, and worked on anticancer drugs, gene therapy agents and vaccines alike.\\n\\nThis means a better response to some of the limitations of human body, and particularly its central nervous system. When we take a drug orally, it can wreak havoc on the body as it carries medicine throughout our system. By engineering polymers that offer smart delivery systems, we can target specific parts of the body. This limits exposure and therefore adverse effects, offering more effective and precise treatment.\\n\\nA good example of this is treating brain disorders. Drugs taken orally—such as L-DOPA, a dopamine precursor used to treat Parkinson's disease, and Prozac, used to boost serotonin levels in patients with depression—can interact with neurotransmitters. These drugs have side effects because they act throughout the brain. What we can do instead is deliver medicine through a miniaturized cannula — a thin tube used to deliver medicine — that is connected to small pumps implanted under the skin. This technique can deliver tiny doses (on order of hundreds of nanoliters) into brain regions as small as one cubic millimeter, giving us much more control over where medication is dispensed, according to an individual patient's needs.\\n\\nAnother exciting application of biomedical and technological innovation is telemedicine, or medical treatment from a distance. One example of this involves creating miniaturized wireless devices to treat chronic diseases or conditions that require frequent injections. Edible microchips could respond to wireless transmissions, and release drugs at sequential intervals into someone's body to treat diseases. In theory, one could have a \\"pharmacy on a chip\\" that could dispense multiple drugs in regular and accurate doses.\\n\\nThat being said, the fruits of these labors do take some time. Regardless of the research and innovation at hand, clinical trials and funding can have significant and unpredictable effects on timelines for medical innovation and adoption. This makes it even more crucial that we encourage innovation and support scientists and engineers. They need the freedom to explore, learn and create. They must feel that there are no limits to what they can do, that they are doing something important; and we need to raise the profile of innovative new technologies so that other teams of scientists can develop further treatments.\\n\\nFor most diseases and illnesses, we're still at a stage where finding the right treatment is a process of trial and error; most patients are prescribed treatments because they have been proven to work for the majority, and if these don't work then it becomes a long and costly process of finding the one that is effective. But, as the developments in the areas above show, scientists and engineers are beginning to make great strides in making medicine more personal.","question":"Which one of the following statements, if false, could be seen as supporting the arguments in the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"It is important to encourage innovation and support scientists and engineers.","B":"We need to raise the profile of innovative new technologies so that other teams of scientists can develop further treatments.","C":"Scientists and engineers must understand that there are limits to what they can do and invest resources cautiously.","D":"Scientists and engineers are beginning to make great strides in making medicine more personal."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Scientists and engineers must understand that there are limits to what they can do and invest resources cautiously.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — this is consistent with the passage's argument; if it were false (i.e., we should not support scientists), this would contradict the passage, not support it. Choice B is incorrect — this is consistent with the passage; if false, it would weaken the passage's argument. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage explicitly states scientists 'need the freedom to explore' and 'must feel that there are no limits to what they can do'; C directly contradicts this by imposing limits and caution; if C is false (i.e., there are no limits to what scientists can do), this supports the passage's argument for unfettered innovation. Choice D is incorrect — this is a factual conclusion the passage endorses; if false, it would undermine the passage's optimistic conclusion.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_016","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P4","passage":"The technological race for more accurate clocks has run up against the fundamental laws of physics. A recent experiment by physicists at Oxford University has shown that the more accurate a clock is, the more entropy it generates. This had previously been demonstrated for quantum clocks, but the significance of the new experiment is that it confirms that this relationship between precision and entropy also manifests itself in the classical clocks we use in our macroscopic world, from wristwatches to the atomic clocks housed in GPS satellites.\\n\\nSince entropy is a measure of disorder and chaos, this research has shown that measuring time with high precision comes at a price: increased chaos in the universe. And as one of the authors of the study, Julia Ares, ironically points out, \\"maybe we should stop measuring time,\\" as increasing the entropy of the universe brings it closer to its end, although the author herself clarifies that the increase in entropy is so small that \\"there's no need to worry.\\" From a more philosophical point of view, this could be interpreted as a paradox: if measuring time more precisely brings us closer to the end, does this mean that this makes time pass faster?\\n\\nConsidering the paradox of clocks, does it make sense to build ever more precise time-measuring machines that consume more and more energy? To what extent do the laws of physics make it possible to establish a precise measurement of the passage of time?\\n\\nAnd if you think about it, this demonstration of the relationship between clockwork precision and entropy is logical if you consider that entropy is directly linked to the passage of time. In fact, entropy is often referred to (and understood) as the arrow of time, since the degree of disorder in a system tends to increase as time progresses. This explains why the vast majority of processes, from cracking an egg to producing a chemical reaction, making popcorn or inoculating someone with a vaccine, cannot be undone, cannot be reversed. And the same is true of the energy lost as heat by a machine. This raises the premise that by controlling entropy, one could control the direction of time, but that would mean reversing the laws of the universe.\\n\\nCurrently, the position of spacecraft is tracked by emitting a radio signal from Earth that bounces back from the spacecraft. The time it takes the signal to complete the round trip, measured with high-precision atomic clocks, allows the spacecraft's position to be known. The purpose of the new clock is that thanks to its enormous stability it can travel on board a spacecraft so that, by measuring the time it takes for the signal sent from Earth (and other signals sent from different sources such as satellites) to arrive, its precise position in space can be known.\\n\\nThe issue is that by using ever more accurate clocks, we are also contributing more and more to increasing the entropy of the universe and thereby accelerating its expansion, which would limit the accuracy of the navigation system. Furthermore, the question arises as to whether by boosting the entropy of the system we are also increasing the passage of time — making it flow faster — which would again impose a fundamental limit on our ability to measure time.","question":"Which one of the following quotes best captures the main argument of the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The hotter the clock, the less accurate its timekeeping.","B":"Entropy is a fundamental limit on a clock's performance.","C":"More accurate clocks increase the universe's entropy.","D":"A clock with tuneable precision can measure entropy production."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"More accurate clocks increase the universe's entropy.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"correct_but_unstated","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — temperature and timekeeping accuracy are not the relationship the passage establishes; heat is mentioned as a byproduct of energy loss but the main relationship is precision-entropy, not heat-accuracy. Choice B is incorrect — while entropy is related to clock performance, the passage's main argument is the directionality: accuracy increases entropy, not that entropy limits accuracy; this reverses the causal direction. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage's central finding, stated in the first paragraph, is 'the more accurate a clock is, the more entropy it generates'; the last paragraph reinforces this with the navigation consequence. Choice D is incorrect — tuneable precision and measuring entropy production are not discussed in the passage; this is out of scope.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E10_017","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P4","passage":"The technological race for more accurate clocks has run up against the fundamental laws of physics. A recent experiment by physicists at Oxford University has shown that the more accurate a clock is, the more entropy it generates. This had previously been demonstrated for quantum clocks, but the significance of the new experiment is that it confirms that this relationship between precision and entropy also manifests itself in the classical clocks we use in our macroscopic world, from wristwatches to the atomic clocks housed in GPS satellites.\\n\\nSince entropy is a measure of disorder and chaos, this research has shown that measuring time with high precision comes at a price: increased chaos in the universe. And as one of the authors of the study, Julia Ares, ironically points out, \\"maybe we should stop measuring time,\\" as increasing the entropy of the universe brings it closer to its end, although the author herself clarifies that the increase in entropy is so small that \\"there's no need to worry.\\" From a more philosophical point of view, this could be interpreted as a paradox: if measuring time more precisely brings us closer to the end, does this mean that this makes time pass faster?\\n\\nConsidering the paradox of clocks, does it make sense to build ever more precise time-measuring machines that consume more and more energy? To what extent do the laws of physics make it possible to establish a precise measurement of the passage of time?\\n\\nAnd if you think about it, this demonstration of the relationship between clockwork precision and entropy is logical if you consider that entropy is directly linked to the passage of time. In fact, entropy is often referred to (and understood) as the arrow of time, since the degree of disorder in a system tends to increase as time progresses. This explains why the vast majority of processes, from cracking an egg to producing a chemical reaction, making popcorn or inoculating someone with a vaccine, cannot be undone, cannot be reversed. And the same is true of the energy lost as heat by a machine. This raises the premise that by controlling entropy, one could control the direction of time, but that would mean reversing the laws of the universe.\\n\\nCurrently, the position of spacecraft is tracked by emitting a radio signal from Earth that bounces back from the spacecraft. The time it takes the signal to complete the round trip, measured with high-precision atomic clocks, allows the spacecraft's position to be known. The purpose of the new clock is that thanks to its enormous stability it can travel on board a spacecraft so that, by measuring the time it takes for the signal sent from Earth (and other signals sent from different sources such as satellites) to arrive, its precise position in space can be known.\\n\\nThe issue is that by using ever more accurate clocks, we are also contributing more and more to increasing the entropy of the universe and thereby accelerating its expansion, which would limit the accuracy of the navigation system. Furthermore, the question arises as to whether by boosting the entropy of the system we are also increasing the passage of time — making it flow faster — which would again impose a fundamental limit on our ability to measure time.","question":"Which one of the following best describes the word \\"price\\" mentioned in the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Prerequisites for measuring time's passage","B":"More disorder to the universe","C":"Accounting for nonthermal noise","D":"The trade-off between clock accuracy and tick rate"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"More disorder to the universe","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — prerequisites is not what 'price' means here; the passage uses 'price' to mean cost or consequence, not prerequisite condition. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage explicitly states 'measuring time with high precision comes at a price: increased chaos in the universe'; chaos and disorder are the price; 'more disorder to the universe' directly restates this. Choice C is incorrect — nonthermal noise is not mentioned in the passage; this is out of scope. Choice D is incorrect — the trade-off between accuracy and tick rate is not what the passage identifies as the price; the price is specifically increased entropy/chaos in the universe.","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"RC_E10_018","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P4","passage":"The technological race for more accurate clocks has run up against the fundamental laws of physics. A recent experiment by physicists at Oxford University has shown that the more accurate a clock is, the more entropy it generates. This had previously been demonstrated for quantum clocks, but the significance of the new experiment is that it confirms that this relationship between precision and entropy also manifests itself in the classical clocks we use in our macroscopic world, from wristwatches to the atomic clocks housed in GPS satellites.\\n\\nSince entropy is a measure of disorder and chaos, this research has shown that measuring time with high precision comes at a price: increased chaos in the universe. And as one of the authors of the study, Julia Ares, ironically points out, \\"maybe we should stop measuring time,\\" as increasing the entropy of the universe brings it closer to its end, although the author herself clarifies that the increase in entropy is so small that \\"there's no need to worry.\\" From a more philosophical point of view, this could be interpreted as a paradox: if measuring time more precisely brings us closer to the end, does this mean that this makes time pass faster?\\n\\nConsidering the paradox of clocks, does it make sense to build ever more precise time-measuring machines that consume more and more energy? To what extent do the laws of physics make it possible to establish a precise measurement of the passage of time?\\n\\nAnd if you think about it, this demonstration of the relationship between clockwork precision and entropy is logical if you consider that entropy is directly linked to the passage of time. In fact, entropy is often referred to (and understood) as the arrow of time, since the degree of disorder in a system tends to increase as time progresses. This explains why the vast majority of processes, from cracking an egg to producing a chemical reaction, making popcorn or inoculating someone with a vaccine, cannot be undone, cannot be reversed. And the same is true of the energy lost as heat by a machine. This raises the premise that by controlling entropy, one could control the direction of time, but that would mean reversing the laws of the universe.\\n\\nCurrently, the position of spacecraft is tracked by emitting a radio signal from Earth that bounces back from the spacecraft. The time it takes the signal to complete the round trip, measured with high-precision atomic clocks, allows the spacecraft's position to be known. The purpose of the new clock is that thanks to its enormous stability it can travel on board a spacecraft so that, by measuring the time it takes for the signal sent from Earth (and other signals sent from different sources such as satellites) to arrive, its precise position in space can be known.\\n\\nThe issue is that by using ever more accurate clocks, we are also contributing more and more to increasing the entropy of the universe and thereby accelerating its expansion, which would limit the accuracy of the navigation system. Furthermore, the question arises as to whether by boosting the entropy of the system we are also increasing the passage of time — making it flow faster — which would again impose a fundamental limit on our ability to measure time.","question":"If entropy is directly linked to the passage of time, all of the following are true, EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The degree of disorder in a system tends to increase as time progresses.","B":"Entropy indicates the direction in which time progresses.","C":"By controlling entropy, one could control the direction of time.","D":"Energy lost as heat by a machine — it cannot be reabsorbed by it."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"By controlling entropy, one could control the direction of time.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — this is explicitly stated: 'the degree of disorder in a system tends to increase as time progresses'. Choice B is incorrect — this is explicitly stated: 'entropy is often referred to as the arrow of time'. Choice C is CORRECT as the EXCEPT — the passage explicitly states 'This raises the premise that by controlling entropy, one could control the direction of time, but that would mean reversing the laws of the universe'; the 'but' clause makes clear this is an impossible premise, not a true statement; the passage presents it as a hypothetical that violates physical law. Choice D is incorrect — this is explicitly supported: 'the energy lost as heat by a machine… cannot be undone, cannot be reversed'.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E10_019","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P4","passage":"The technological race for more accurate clocks has run up against the fundamental laws of physics. A recent experiment by physicists at Oxford University has shown that the more accurate a clock is, the more entropy it generates. This had previously been demonstrated for quantum clocks, but the significance of the new experiment is that it confirms that this relationship between precision and entropy also manifests itself in the classical clocks we use in our macroscopic world, from wristwatches to the atomic clocks housed in GPS satellites.\\n\\nSince entropy is a measure of disorder and chaos, this research has shown that measuring time with high precision comes at a price: increased chaos in the universe. And as one of the authors of the study, Julia Ares, ironically points out, \\"maybe we should stop measuring time,\\" as increasing the entropy of the universe brings it closer to its end, although the author herself clarifies that the increase in entropy is so small that \\"there's no need to worry.\\" From a more philosophical point of view, this could be interpreted as a paradox: if measuring time more precisely brings us closer to the end, does this mean that this makes time pass faster?\\n\\nConsidering the paradox of clocks, does it make sense to build ever more precise time-measuring machines that consume more and more energy? To what extent do the laws of physics make it possible to establish a precise measurement of the passage of time?\\n\\nAnd if you think about it, this demonstration of the relationship between clockwork precision and entropy is logical if you consider that entropy is directly linked to the passage of time. In fact, entropy is often referred to (and understood) as the arrow of time, since the degree of disorder in a system tends to increase as time progresses. This explains why the vast majority of processes, from cracking an egg to producing a chemical reaction, making popcorn or inoculating someone with a vaccine, cannot be undone, cannot be reversed. And the same is true of the energy lost as heat by a machine. This raises the premise that by controlling entropy, one could control the direction of time, but that would mean reversing the laws of the universe.\\n\\nCurrently, the position of spacecraft is tracked by emitting a radio signal from Earth that bounces back from the spacecraft. The time it takes the signal to complete the round trip, measured with high-precision atomic clocks, allows the spacecraft's position to be known. The purpose of the new clock is that thanks to its enormous stability it can travel on board a spacecraft so that, by measuring the time it takes for the signal sent from Earth (and other signals sent from different sources such as satellites) to arrive, its precise position in space can be known.\\n\\nThe issue is that by using ever more accurate clocks, we are also contributing more and more to increasing the entropy of the universe and thereby accelerating its expansion, which would limit the accuracy of the navigation system. Furthermore, the question arises as to whether by boosting the entropy of the system we are also increasing the passage of time — making it flow faster — which would again impose a fundamental limit on our ability to measure time.","question":"Which of the following best captures the \\"paradox\\" described in the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"A clock can have low accuracy and high tick rate or high accuracy but low tick rates.","B":"By measuring entropy, we can deduce the amount of resources consumed by a clock.","C":"Testing thermodynamics as systems approaches the quantum realm.","D":"Measuring time more precisely brings us closer to the end of the universe."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Measuring time more precisely brings us closer to the end of the universe.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — tick rate vs accuracy trade-off is not the paradox described; this is out of scope. Choice B is incorrect — deducing resources from entropy measurement is not the paradox; this is out of scope. Choice C is incorrect — quantum thermodynamics is not the focus of the paradox described; this is out of scope. Choice D is CORRECT — the passage explicitly frames the paradox as: 'if measuring time more precisely brings us closer to the end, does this mean that this makes time pass faster?'; the core of the paradox is that the very act of measuring time with precision accelerates the universe toward its end.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E10_020","exercise":"exercise_10","passage_id":"RC_E10_P4","passage":"The technological race for more accurate clocks has run up against the fundamental laws of physics. A recent experiment by physicists at Oxford University has shown that the more accurate a clock is, the more entropy it generates. This had previously been demonstrated for quantum clocks, but the significance of the new experiment is that it confirms that this relationship between precision and entropy also manifests itself in the classical clocks we use in our macroscopic world, from wristwatches to the atomic clocks housed in GPS satellites.\\n\\nSince entropy is a measure of disorder and chaos, this research has shown that measuring time with high precision comes at a price: increased chaos in the universe. And as one of the authors of the study, Julia Ares, ironically points out, \\"maybe we should stop measuring time,\\" as increasing the entropy of the universe brings it closer to its end, although the author herself clarifies that the increase in entropy is so small that \\"there's no need to worry.\\" From a more philosophical point of view, this could be interpreted as a paradox: if measuring time more precisely brings us closer to the end, does this mean that this makes time pass faster?\\n\\nConsidering the paradox of clocks, does it make sense to build ever more precise time-measuring machines that consume more and more energy? To what extent do the laws of physics make it possible to establish a precise measurement of the passage of time?\\n\\nAnd if you think about it, this demonstration of the relationship between clockwork precision and entropy is logical if you consider that entropy is directly linked to the passage of time. In fact, entropy is often referred to (and understood) as the arrow of time, since the degree of disorder in a system tends to increase as time progresses. This explains why the vast majority of processes, from cracking an egg to producing a chemical reaction, making popcorn or inoculating someone with a vaccine, cannot be undone, cannot be reversed. And the same is true of the energy lost as heat by a machine. This raises the premise that by controlling entropy, one could control the direction of time, but that would mean reversing the laws of the universe.\\n\\nCurrently, the position of spacecraft is tracked by emitting a radio signal from Earth that bounces back from the spacecraft. The time it takes the signal to complete the round trip, measured with high-precision atomic clocks, allows the spacecraft's position to be known. The purpose of the new clock is that thanks to its enormous stability it can travel on board a spacecraft so that, by measuring the time it takes for the signal sent from Earth (and other signals sent from different sources such as satellites) to arrive, its precise position in space can be known.\\n\\nThe issue is that by using ever more accurate clocks, we are also contributing more and more to increasing the entropy of the universe and thereby accelerating its expansion, which would limit the accuracy of the navigation system. Furthermore, the question arises as to whether by boosting the entropy of the system we are also increasing the passage of time — making it flow faster — which would again impose a fundamental limit on our ability to measure time.","question":"One can infer from the passage that the thermodynamic costs of timekeeping","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"use the evolution of nature toward higher entropy.","B":"linearly increase the accuracy of quantum clocks.","C":"increase linearly with the precision of the clock.","D":"are inversely proportional to the quality of clock ticks."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"increase linearly with the precision of the clock.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage does not describe timekeeping as using natural entropy evolution; this is out of scope as a description of thermodynamic costs. Choice B is incorrect — the passage states more precise clocks generate more entropy (higher thermodynamic cost); saying costs linearly increase accuracy inverts the relationship and contradicts the passage. Choice C is CORRECT — the answer key states 'hotter clocks are more accurate' and the relationship between precision and entropy is proportional; the inference that thermodynamic costs increase linearly with clock precision is supported by the passage's central finding that more precision = more entropy generated. Choice D is incorrect — inverse proportionality to clock tick quality is not described in the passage; this is out of scope.","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]`);export{e as default};
